1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,249
Antarctica's ice is so thick that, in places,

2
00:00:03,249 --> 00:00:08,884
the ground hidden beneath it lies more than two and a half kilometers below sea level.

3
00:00:09,384 --> 00:00:10,883
And there's a lot of it.

4
00:00:10,883 --> 00:00:13,880
Melt every scrap of ice sitting on Earth's land,

5
00:00:13,880 --> 00:00:16,753
and the oceans rise by roughly seventy meters.

6
00:00:16,753 --> 00:00:19,438
That's taller than a twenty-story building.

7
00:00:19,938 --> 00:00:21,468
So what if it all melted?

8
00:00:21,468 --> 00:00:24,344
Every glacier and every ice sheet, all at once.

9
00:00:24,344 --> 00:00:28,689
Where would the new coastline be, and what would we find under the ice?

10
00:00:32,933 --> 00:00:34,131
Here are the rules.

11
00:00:34,131 --> 00:00:39,045
We flip a switch, and every bit of ice sitting on land turns to water at once.

12
00:00:39,045 --> 00:00:41,125
The meltwater pours into the sea.

13
00:00:41,125 --> 00:00:43,834
Everything else about Earth stays the same.

14
00:00:44,334 --> 00:00:46,535
Most of that ice is in two places.

15
00:00:46,535 --> 00:00:51,195
Antarctica's ice sheet covers almost fourteen million square kilometers.

16
00:00:51,195 --> 00:00:55,532
That's about the size of the United States and Mexico put together.

17
00:00:55,532 --> 00:00:58,638
On average, it's more than two kilometers thick.

18
00:00:59,138 --> 00:01:03,471
Greenland's ice sheet is smaller, about three times the size of Texas,

19
00:01:03,471 --> 00:01:06,690
but in places it's more than three kilometers thick.

20
00:01:06,690 --> 00:01:07,495
Between them,

21
00:01:07,495 --> 00:01:12,570
these two sheets of ice hold more than two thirds of all the fresh water on Earth.

22
00:01:13,070 --> 00:01:17,155
In the real world, melting all of this would take thousands of years.

23
00:01:17,155 --> 00:01:18,221
We'll get to that.

24
00:01:18,221 --> 00:01:20,174
But first, let's flip the switch.

25
00:01:21,033 --> 00:01:22,995
So how high does the water go?

26
00:01:22,995 --> 00:01:28,358
Antarctica alone holds enough ice to raise the oceans by about fifty-eight meters.

27
00:01:28,858 --> 00:01:31,709
Greenland adds about seven and a half meters more.

28
00:01:32,209 --> 00:01:36,195
And every other glacier on the planet, in the Alps, the Himalayas,

29
00:01:36,195 --> 00:01:39,999
the Andes and Alaska, adds up to only about a third of a meter.

30
00:01:39,999 --> 00:01:44,829
Hundreds of thousands of glaciers, and the water wouldn't even reach your knees.

31
00:01:45,329 --> 00:01:48,814
Add it all up, and you get roughly sixty-six meters.

32
00:01:48,814 --> 00:01:49,350
The U.S.

33
00:01:49,350 --> 00:01:53,102
Geological Survey gives a round figure of about seventy.

34
00:01:53,102 --> 00:01:57,323
Either way, the sea climbs higher than a twenty-story building.

35
00:01:58,167 --> 00:02:00,165
Notice what's missing from that list.

36
00:02:00,165 --> 00:02:01,570
The ice at the North Pole.

37
00:02:02,070 --> 00:02:03,483
Arctic sea ice floats.

38
00:02:03,483 --> 00:02:05,861
Like an ice cube in a glass of water,

39
00:02:05,861 --> 00:02:09,138
it already pushes aside its own weight in seawater.

40
00:02:09,138 --> 00:02:11,901
So when it melts, the level hardly changes.

41
00:02:12,401 --> 00:02:13,767
Hardly, but not zero.

42
00:02:13,767 --> 00:02:17,931
Meltwater is fresh, and slightly less dense than salty seawater,

43
00:02:17,931 --> 00:02:20,144
so it takes up a little more room.

44
00:02:20,144 --> 00:02:27,041
NASA estimates the effect is only about three percent of what you'd get from the same ice melting on land.

45
00:02:27,541 --> 00:02:32,604
So the Arctic Ocean could lose all of its ice, and the beaches would barely notice.

46
00:02:32,604 --> 00:02:35,227
The real danger is the ice sitting on rock.

47
00:02:37,967 --> 00:02:42,092
Seventy meters later, the map of the world looks very different.

48
00:02:42,092 --> 00:02:46,474
National Geographic mapped this exact scenario, and in the Americas,

49
00:02:46,474 --> 00:02:47,957
the damage is enormous.

50
00:02:48,457 --> 00:02:50,589
Almost all of Florida disappears,

51
00:02:50,589 --> 00:02:53,949
along with the Gulf Coast and the Atlantic seaboard.

52
00:02:53,949 --> 00:02:59,248
Miami is gone completely, and the low-lying parts of New York and Boston go under.

53
00:02:59,748 --> 00:03:04,326
On the West Coast, San Francisco's hills become a cluster of islands,

54
00:03:04,326 --> 00:03:07,976
and California's Central Valley turns into a giant bay.

55
00:03:08,476 --> 00:03:13,113
Further south, the Amazon basin becomes an arm of the Atlantic Ocean,

56
00:03:13,113 --> 00:03:15,129
and Buenos Aires is swallowed.

57
00:03:15,967 --> 00:03:17,589
In Europe, London is lost.

58
00:03:17,589 --> 00:03:18,400
So is Venice.

59
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:21,957
The Netherlands, and most of Denmark, slip under the sea.

60
00:03:22,457 --> 00:03:27,060
Across the Mediterranean, the water reaches Alexandria, and even Cairo,

61
00:03:27,060 --> 00:03:31,275
which today sits more than a hundred and fifty kilometers inland.

62
00:03:32,133 --> 00:03:37,499
In Asia, nearly all of Bangladesh disappears, along with much of India's coast.

63
00:03:37,499 --> 00:03:38,110
In China,

64
00:03:38,110 --> 00:03:44,765
National Geographic estimated that land now home to around six hundred million people would flood.

65
00:03:45,265 --> 00:03:46,718
That includes Shanghai,

66
00:03:46,718 --> 00:03:51,834
a city whose center has already sunk more than three meters since the late 1800s,

67
00:03:51,834 --> 00:03:56,381
and which sits behind seawalls typically just five to seven meters high.

68
00:03:56,881 --> 00:04:01,027
Africa, as it turns out, loses less land than most other continents,

69
00:04:01,027 --> 00:04:03,282
although its coastlines still shrink.

70
00:04:06,033 --> 00:04:07,884
After the flood comes the heat.

71
00:04:07,884 --> 00:04:08,839
Ice is a mirror.

72
00:04:08,839 --> 00:04:13,496
Fresh snow can bounce back around ninety percent of the sunlight that hits it.

73
00:04:13,997 --> 00:04:15,752
Open ocean is the opposite.

74
00:04:15,752 --> 00:04:20,235
It reflects less than a tenth of the sunlight, and soaks up the rest.

75
00:04:20,735 --> 00:04:25,261
Swap millions of square kilometers of white for dark water and rock,

76
00:04:25,261 --> 00:04:27,656
and Earth absorbs far more sunshine.

77
00:04:27,656 --> 00:04:32,315
That warms the planet, which melts more ice, which warms it even more.

78
00:04:32,315 --> 00:04:35,309
Scientists call this the ice-albedo feedback.

79
00:04:35,809 --> 00:04:40,984
And in reality, a world warm enough to melt all of its ice would already be hot.

80
00:04:40,984 --> 00:04:47,193
National Geographic put that world's average temperature at around twenty-seven degrees Celsius.

81
00:04:47,193 --> 00:04:48,939
Today, it's about fourteen.

82
00:04:49,800 --> 00:04:51,525
Then there are the oceans.

83
00:04:51,525 --> 00:04:55,505
Around Antarctica, cold, salty water sinks to the sea floor,

84
00:04:55,505 --> 00:04:58,755
and drives a slow current through the deep ocean.

85
00:04:59,255 --> 00:05:04,447
Fresh meltwater is lighter, so it floats on top, and the sinking slows down.

86
00:05:04,447 --> 00:05:07,316
One study found that under high emissions,

87
00:05:07,316 --> 00:05:11,688
this deep circulation could slow by about forty percent by 2050.

88
00:05:12,188 --> 00:05:15,216
Pour in an entire ice sheet's worth of fresh water,

89
00:05:15,216 --> 00:05:17,294
and that slow conveyor could stall.

90
00:05:17,294 --> 00:05:19,550
Nutrients would get stuck in the deep,

91
00:05:19,550 --> 00:05:22,340
and less would reach the life near the surface.

92
00:05:23,200 --> 00:05:25,460
Quick favour, before we go any further:

93
00:05:25,460 --> 00:05:28,242
if you're enjoying this, tap like and subscribe.

94
00:05:28,242 --> 00:05:30,038
It genuinely helps the channel.

95
00:05:30,538 --> 00:05:32,720
Even the length of your day changes.

96
00:05:32,720 --> 00:05:36,964
As meltwater flows away from the poles and spreads toward the equator,

97
00:05:36,964 --> 00:05:39,449
Earth's mass moves farther from its axis.

98
00:05:39,949 --> 00:05:43,455
It's like a figure skater spinning with her arms out.

99
00:05:43,455 --> 00:05:44,778
The spin slows down.

100
00:05:44,778 --> 00:05:55,297
Researchers estimate that melting ice and rising seas have been stretching the day by about one point three three milliseconds per century since the year 2000.

101
00:05:55,797 --> 00:05:56,461
Tiny, sure.

102
00:05:56,461 --> 00:05:58,090
But if emissions stay high,

103
00:05:58,090 --> 00:06:04,004
they think this could outdo the Moon's effect on the length of our day by the end of this century.

104
00:06:06,767 --> 00:06:09,150
Now, fast-forward ten thousand years.

105
00:06:09,150 --> 00:06:11,663
Something strange is happening on land.

106
00:06:11,663 --> 00:06:12,436
It's rising.

107
00:06:12,936 --> 00:06:17,806
Ice sheets are so heavy that they press the Earth's crust down into the hot,

108
00:06:17,806 --> 00:06:19,792
slowly flowing rock beneath it.

109
00:06:19,792 --> 00:06:22,676
Take the ice away, and the land springs back.

110
00:06:22,676 --> 00:06:24,086
But very, very slowly.

111
00:06:24,586 --> 00:06:26,965
We know, because it's still happening.

112
00:06:26,965 --> 00:06:29,596
About ten thousand five hundred years ago,

113
00:06:29,596 --> 00:06:33,290
an ice sheet three kilometers thick melted off Scandinavia.

114
00:06:33,290 --> 00:06:33,666
Today,

115
00:06:33,666 --> 00:06:39,365
the coast of the Gulf of Bothnia is still rising by about eight or nine millimeters a year.

116
00:06:39,865 --> 00:06:42,014
In Finland's Kvarken archipelago,

117
00:06:42,014 --> 00:06:45,921
that adds about one square kilometer of new land every year.

118
00:06:45,921 --> 00:06:50,284
And the crust there still has about a hundred meters left to climb.

119
00:06:50,784 --> 00:06:52,169
On Sweden's High Coast,

120
00:06:52,169 --> 00:06:58,852
the highest shoreline from the end of the Ice Age now sits two hundred and eighty-six meters above today's sea.

121
00:06:59,700 --> 00:07:02,519
Now imagine that across all of Antarctica.

122
00:07:02,519 --> 00:07:06,479
With its twenty-seven million cubic kilometers of ice gone,

123
00:07:06,479 --> 00:07:09,231
the land underneath finally sees the sky.

124
00:07:09,731 --> 00:07:11,903
And it isn't one neat continent.

125
00:07:11,903 --> 00:07:16,246
Scientists expect East Antarctica to be a single large landmass,

126
00:07:16,246 --> 00:07:20,793
and West Antarctica to be a scatter of rugged, mountainous islands.

127
00:07:21,293 --> 00:07:26,307
That's because so much of West Antarctica's rock floor lies below sea level.

128
00:07:26,307 --> 00:07:29,672
Its deepest known basin, the Byrd Subglacial Basin,

129
00:07:29,672 --> 00:07:33,433
sits more than two and a half kilometers below the waves.

130
00:07:33,933 --> 00:07:37,345
Over in East Antarctica, beneath the Denman Glacier,

131
00:07:37,345 --> 00:07:42,004
a canyon plunges more than three and a half kilometers below sea level.

132
00:07:42,004 --> 00:07:44,628
It's the deepest point on land anywhere.

133
00:07:44,628 --> 00:07:48,828
The shore of the Dead Sea, the lowest dry land you can stand on,

134
00:07:48,828 --> 00:07:51,387
is only about four hundred meters down.

135
00:07:51,887 --> 00:07:54,876
Over thousands of years, as the rock rebounds,

136
00:07:54,876 --> 00:07:57,800
some of those islands would rise and join up.

137
00:07:57,800 --> 00:08:02,868
But for a very long time, the new Antarctica would be part land, and part sea.

138
00:08:05,633 --> 00:08:06,808
So, back to reality.

139
00:08:06,808 --> 00:08:09,863
How long would it actually take to melt all of this?

140
00:08:10,363 --> 00:08:11,054
Not a day.

141
00:08:11,054 --> 00:08:16,853
In 2024, NASA measured the global sea rising about six millimeters in a single year.

142
00:08:16,853 --> 00:08:22,307
At that pace, reaching seventy meters would take roughly twelve thousand years.

143
00:08:22,807 --> 00:08:25,812
Melting speeds up as the planet warms, of course.

144
00:08:25,812 --> 00:08:30,595
Some scientists think melting it all would take more than five thousand years.

145
00:08:31,095 --> 00:08:31,668
In 2015,

146
00:08:31,668 --> 00:08:38,679
a team led by Ricarda Winkelmann modeled what happens if we burn all the fossil fuel we can reach.

147
00:08:38,679 --> 00:08:43,615
Antarctica ends up almost ice-free, and for the first thousand years,

148
00:08:43,615 --> 00:08:46,977
the sea rises about three meters every century.

149
00:08:47,477 --> 00:08:52,611
And their model suggested West Antarctica could tip into decline with far less:

150
00:08:52,611 --> 00:08:56,964
around six hundred to eight hundred billion extra tonnes of carbon.

151
00:08:59,700 --> 00:09:00,827
And here's the thing.

152
00:09:00,827 --> 00:09:02,277
Earth has done this before.

153
00:09:02,777 --> 00:09:05,852
About three million years ago, in the Pliocene,

154
00:09:05,852 --> 00:09:09,843
carbon dioxide was last above four hundred parts per million.

155
00:09:09,843 --> 00:09:13,114
The planet was about three to four degrees warmer,

156
00:09:13,114 --> 00:09:15,339
Greenland was largely free of ice,

157
00:09:15,339 --> 00:09:18,676
and the seas stood fifteen to twenty meters higher.

158
00:09:18,676 --> 00:09:21,947
Today, carbon dioxide is above four hundred again.

159
00:09:22,447 --> 00:09:25,230
Go back more than thirty-four million years,

160
00:09:25,230 --> 00:09:28,456
and there was little or no ice at the poles at all.

161
00:09:28,456 --> 00:09:30,923
West Antarctica was covered in forests.

162
00:09:31,423 --> 00:09:34,871
Then the planet cooled, ice began to build on Antarctica,

163
00:09:34,871 --> 00:09:36,505
and over millions of years,

164
00:09:36,505 --> 00:09:39,227
the seas settled near the coastlines we know.

165
00:09:40,067 --> 00:09:41,311
But here's the twist.

166
00:09:41,311 --> 00:09:45,635
If Greenland melted, the sea right next to Greenland would actually fall.

167
00:09:46,135 --> 00:09:49,954
The ice sheet weighs around three thousand trillion tonnes.

168
00:09:49,954 --> 00:09:52,220
That much mass has its own gravity,

169
00:09:52,220 --> 00:09:54,745
and it tugs the nearby ocean toward it,

170
00:09:54,745 --> 00:09:56,946
piling water up against its coast.

171
00:09:57,446 --> 00:09:59,674
Melt the ice, and that pull fades.

172
00:09:59,674 --> 00:10:01,117
The water slumps away.

173
00:10:01,117 --> 00:10:04,001
And the land, freed of its weight, rises up.

174
00:10:04,001 --> 00:10:11,212
Models from Harvard researchers suggest sea level near Greenland's coast would drop by twenty to fifty meters.

175
00:10:11,712 --> 00:10:13,408
So where does the water go?

176
00:10:13,408 --> 00:10:13,973
Far away.

177
00:10:13,973 --> 00:10:17,428
More than about two thousand kilometers from Greenland,

178
00:10:17,428 --> 00:10:20,129
the sea rises more than the global average.

179
00:10:20,629 --> 00:10:22,637
Antarctica works the same way.

180
00:10:22,637 --> 00:10:26,788
Its melt would raise the seas most in places far to the north:

181
00:10:26,788 --> 00:10:30,939
across the Pacific, the Indian Ocean and the western Atlantic.

182
00:10:30,939 --> 00:10:34,555
Each ice sheet hits the far side of the world hardest.

183
00:10:35,055 --> 00:10:36,722
And this isn't just theory.

184
00:10:36,722 --> 00:10:41,538
Satellites measuring the sea surface since the 1990s have matched the pattern.

185
00:10:42,400 --> 00:10:46,177
So if all the ice melted, the sea would rise about seventy meters,

186
00:10:46,177 --> 00:10:50,297
Antarctica would break into islands, your day would get a little longer,

187
00:10:50,297 --> 00:10:53,960
and the water would be lowest right next to the ice that melted.

188
00:10:54,460 --> 00:10:58,118
If the coastline moved seventy meters uphill, where would you move?

189
00:10:58,118 --> 00:10:59,429
Tell me in the comments.
